import{_ as o,C as a,o as r,c as n,j as t,a as i,E as h,a6 as d}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const l="/screenshots/vscode-run-control.png",k=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Pause and step from the editor","description":"Pause a test run Piwi started from VS Code or a JetBrains IDE, step it call by call, run it to the cursor, resume or finish it, with the paused line marked in the editor, your breakpoints applied as you change them, and a headless test's page shown as an image.","frontmatter":{"title":"Pause and step from the editor","description":"Pause a test run Piwi started from VS Code or a JetBrains IDE, step it call by call, run it to the cursor, resume or finish it, with the paused line marked in the editor, your breakpoints applied as you change them, and a headless test's page shown as an image.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/features/editor-run-control.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/features/editor-run-control.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Pause and step from the editor | Piwi Dashboard"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"Pause a test run Piwi started from VS Code or a JetBrains IDE, step it call by call, run it to the cursor, resume or finish it, with the paused line marked in the editor, your breakpoints applied as you change them, and a headless test's page shown as an image."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/editor-run-control.md","filePath":"features/editor-run-control.md","lastUpdated":1793107322000}`),u={name:"features/editor-run-control.md"};function p(c,e,g,w,m,b){const s=a("Needs");return r(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"pause-and-step-from-the-editor",tabindex:"-1"},[i("Pause and step from the editor "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#pause-and-step-from-the-editor","aria-label":'Permalink to "Pause and step from the editor"'},"​")],-1)),h(s,{reporter:"",fixtures:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>A test run the <a href="./editors.html">editor extensions</a> start can be paused, stepped and resumed from the editor, with Piwi&#39;s own commands: no debug session starts, and the IDE&#39;s debugger works as usual beside it. The editor marks the line the test is stopped at as a debugger marks its execution point, and the breakpoints you set or remove reach the run while it goes on. The <a href="./editor-breakpoints.html#pause-a-run">pause bar</a> in the page answers the same stops.</p><h2 id="the-controls" tabindex="-1">The controls <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-controls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The controls&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>VS Code</th><th>JetBrains IDEs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pause</strong></td><td>While a test runs: stops it before its next call that acts on, reads or checks the page</td><td>none</td><td>Resume Program&#39;s, <code>F9</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Resume</strong></td><td>Runs the stopped test on to the next breakpoint</td><td><code>F5</code></td><td>Resume Program&#39;s, <code>F9</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Step</strong></td><td>Runs the call and stops before the next one, breakpoint or not</td><td><code>F10</code></td><td>Step Over&#39;s, <code>F8</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Run to cursor</strong></td><td>Stops at the first call on the cursor&#39;s line, or at a breakpoint before it</td><td><code>Ctrl+F10</code></td><td>Run to Cursor&#39;s, <code>Alt+F9</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Finish</strong></td><td>Runs the test on without stopping at a breakpoint again in this attempt</td><td><code>Shift+F11</code></td><td>Step Out&#39;s, <code>Shift+F8</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stop</strong></td><td>Ends the run&#39;s process</td><td><code>Shift+F5</code></td><td>the Run tool window&#39;s Stop</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The keys are the IDE&#39;s own debugger keys: VS Code&#39;s defaults, and in a JetBrains IDE the shortcuts of those debugger actions in your keymap (the default keymap&#39;s shown). They are Piwi&#39;s only while their control applies and no debug session runs; otherwise they do what they always do. In a JetBrains keymap that gives one of them to another action too (the VSCode keymap&#39;s <code>F5</code>, NetBeans&#39; <code>F4</code>), Piwi&#39;s goes first while it applies. On macOS, <code>Cmd+F10</code> runs to the cursor in VS Code. <strong>Pause</strong> has no key in VS Code, since it applies for as long as the test runs: bind one in <strong>Preferences: Open Keyboard Shortcuts</strong> (search <code>piwi</code>), where the others change too. There, <code>F5</code> pressed again within two seconds of an answer waits for the next stop rather than start a debug session.</p><ul><li><strong>In VS Code</strong>, the controls sit in the status bar beside the run&#39;s progress: <strong>Pause</strong> and <strong>Stop</strong> while the test runs (<code>Pausing…</code> until it stops), <strong>Resume</strong>, <strong>Step</strong>, <strong>Finish</strong> and <strong>Stop</strong> while it is stopped. The palette has them while they apply: <strong>Piwi: Pause the run</strong>, <strong>Piwi: Resume the run</strong>, <strong>Piwi: Step to the next call</strong>, <strong>Piwi: Run to the cursor</strong>, <strong>Piwi: Finish the test without stopping again</strong>, <strong>Piwi: Stop the run</strong> and <strong>Piwi: Show the paused page</strong>.</li><li><strong>In a JetBrains IDE</strong>, they are under <strong>Tools → Piwi</strong>, enabled while they apply, and first in the status bar item&#39;s menu, which reads <code>Piwi: paused · checkout.page.ts:12</code> while a test is stopped.</li></ul><p><strong>Pause</strong> stops every run Piwi started in this window (in a JetBrains IDE, this project), each of its workers before its next call that Playwright&#39;s debugger stops at, the calls <a href="./editor-breakpoints.html#limits">Breakpoints in the browser</a> lists: a long call or a <code>waitForTimeout</code> finishes first, and a worker between tests stops at the next test&#39;s first call. A test that never stops, under CI or Playwright&#39;s own debugger, says why.</p><h2 id="where-the-test-stands" tabindex="-1">Where the test stands <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-test-stands" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the test stands&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a test stops, at a breakpoint, after <strong>Step</strong> or <strong>Pause</strong>, on the line it was run to, or at <code>page.pause()</code>, the editor opens the file of the call about to run, a spec or a page object, with the cursor on its line, and marks the line: an arrow in the gutter, the debugger&#39;s execution point color, and above the line what is about to run, as Playwright names the call, with the controls:</p><p><code>Paused · Fill &quot;MORNING10&quot; getByLabel(&#39;Coupon code&#39;) · attempt 2</code> Resume Step Run to cursor Finish</p><p><code>attempt 2</code> shows on a retry. In VS Code the line is a CodeLens, and the hover on the paused line has the same controls; the color is <code>piwi.executionPointBackground</code>. In a JetBrains IDE the line is an inlay whose words are links, the arrow&#39;s click lists the controls, and the color is <strong>Settings → Editor → Color Scheme → Piwi → Line a run is stopped at</strong>. The mark goes when the test goes on, from the editor or the pause bar, ends, or closes its page.</p><figure><img src="'+l+'" alt="VS Code: a test paused at a page object&#39;s line, the controls above it and in the status bar"><figcaption>A run paused before it types the coupon.</figcaption></figure><p>With several workers stopped, each has its own mark, and the controls above a line act on its worker; a key or a command acts on the one stopped at the cursor&#39;s line, else asks which. A control answers the stop it was shown for: when the test went on meanwhile (a key pressed twice), it says so and asks nothing.</p><p>The run&#39;s code is your files as they stood when it started. The mark follows the lines you add or remove above it, and <strong>Run to cursor</strong> in an edited file runs to the line the run has for the cursor&#39;s; on a line written since, it says the running test has none.</p><h2 id="a-headless-run" tabindex="-1">A headless run <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-headless-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A headless run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A run Piwi starts with breakpoints is headed, so the pause bar answers its stops too. One started without runs as its config says, headless by default: when you pause it, or it reaches a breakpoint set since, no bar shows, and the editor shows an image of the page instead, as it stood when the test stopped: in VS Code in a <strong>Piwi: paused page</strong> tab beside the editor, in a JetBrains IDE in the <strong>Paused Page</strong> tab of the Piwi tool window. It is taken again at each such stop; <strong>Piwi: Show the paused page</strong> (<strong>Tools → Piwi → Show the Paused Page</strong>) asks for it at any stop.</p><p>Whoever answers it, a stop counts as one at the bar: the test&#39;s duration leaves it out, and the dashboard&#39;s timelines draw it as a <strong>Paused</strong> band, with why it stopped (<a href="./editor-breakpoints.html#pause-a-run">Breakpoints in the browser</a>).</p><h2 id="breakpoints-while-the-run-goes-on" tabindex="-1">Breakpoints while the run goes on <a class="header-anchor" href="#breakpoints-while-the-run-goes-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Breakpoints while the run goes on&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A breakpoint you add, remove, enable or disable applies to the run from its next call, in each of its workers and in those Playwright starts after a failure. One that only moves because you edited the lines above it keeps the line the run has, and one you set in an edited file applies to that line of the run&#39;s code. The breakpoints that count are those a run&#39;s start reads: line breakpoints in script files under a Playwright config&#39;s folder, while <code>piwi.breakpoints</code> (<strong>Pause the runs Piwi starts at the editor&#39;s breakpoints</strong> in a JetBrains IDE) is on. Turning the setting off lets the run go on without them.</p><h2 id="how-it-works" tabindex="-1">How it works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it works&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With a reporter newer than 0.59.0, each worker of a run Piwi starts keeps a connection to the editor service while it runs, through a socket on your machine that the service opens, readable by you only, named in the run&#39;s environment as <code>PIWI_RUN_CHANNEL</code>. The worker says which test it runs and where it stops; the editor answers with the controls. A run whose connection does not open, or closes, pauses as it would without it. Stopping the editor service, or closing the IDE, closes the connections: a stop with no pause bar then goes on, and one the bar shows waits there for its answer.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Runs Piwi started only.</strong> A run started in a terminal, or by the IDE&#39;s own Playwright run configuration, has no controls in the editor; with <strong>Run the tests in the IDE&#39;s Playwright runner</strong> on, a JetBrains IDE&#39;s runs keep them, their Rerun, Rerun Failed Tests and Debug included.</li><li><strong>No mark on a breakpoint that never stops.</strong> A breakpoint on a line whose calls Playwright&#39;s debugger never stops at, or with no Playwright call, looks like any other and does nothing.</li><li><strong>Playwright calls, not JavaScript.</strong> <strong>Step</strong> goes from one Playwright call to the next; stepping through the test&#39;s own code stays with the IDE&#39;s debugger.</li><li><strong>One image a stop.</strong> The paused page is a still image, not a live view: it does not follow what you do in a headed browser.</li><li><strong>No image when the page is unknown.</strong> A stop at a call other than a locator&#39;s, such as a navigation or a key press, in a browser context with several pages has no image: the worker cannot tell which of them the call is on. Such a stop also ends when any page of that context closes.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./editor-breakpoints.html">Breakpoints in the browser</a>: the breakpoints a run starts with, and the pause bar.</li><li><a href="./editor-runs.html">Runs from the editor</a>: the commands that start a run, and how the editor follows it.</li><li><a href="./editors.html">Editor extensions</a>: what the editor shows from your suite, in both editors.</li></ul>',24))])}const y=o(u,[["render",p]]);export{k as __pageData,y as default};
